+## Key v2, amendment 1 (added 2026-11-18, after the Claude-v2-A reading, before any further reading is requested)
+
+**Why.** The Claude-v2-A reading exposed places where key v2 applied its own `slots_specific` rule unevenly (`GAP_INSTRUMENT_CRASH`, `cplay:UNKNOWN`), one judgement call (`taf:UNKNOWN`), one spelling (`<value>`), and an open question about derived attributes (`covers_effect`). Changing the key to match that reader would fit the key to a reader. So rules were written first and applied to all 15 terms, and the owner decided them before the next reading.
+
+**Text.** `KEY_V2_AMENDMENT_1.md` (revision 1), sha256 `09dcec7ce977078dfd8bee70afb5d4b999e8903da7b5f8ed4eea07e6c2947184`. Drafted by the Claude-v2-A model after it had seen key v2 and its own score; not independent. The owner fixed S3 as **S3-out** (a slot is an input degree of freedom; an attribute computed after construction from the parameters carries no new information) and accepted S1, S2, ND, N1, C1, K1 as drafted. `amendment_options/OPTIONS.md` then found two places where the patch as received did not follow its own text (T14 under a literal S2; T01 under a literal S1(b)). Revision 1 closes both by rule: **R-a**, each collected name is tested for exclusivity by the tie it was collected under (presence for a field a record has, requirement for a field a rule requires); **R-b**, S1(b) collects the fields of the term's own record only, and does not open a record built beside it or held in one of its fields. The patch as received (`amendment_options/as_received/`, sha256 `8ea98d4cd6cc4702b239a626b4f45c54ad1174f209d8a03ce70fe65fa75452bb`) is superseded, not adopted.
+
+**Amended key.** `scoring/key_v2_a1.json`, sha256 `b0a19e82ea57f9ee4e5ff406ad84cbb6ee7c85684f46ddb59f1df5773edf0928`. `key_v2.json` is unchanged (sha256 `c88a910a03a6456900efabd130c57a19f1a0133c9566ebce34ae0b6e813b18d1`). Changes, `slots_specific` only: T09 `taf:UNKNOWN` `[]` -> undetermined; T10 `taf:GAP_INSTRUMENT_CRASH` six coverage fields -> `[missing_path]`; T12 `cplay:UNKNOWN` `[reason, triggers]` -> `[triggers]`. T14 keeps `[sigma, basis]` (R-a) and T01 keeps `[]` (R-b). Pair `UNKNOWN | UNKNOWN` becomes n/d under `specific`, so a1 `specific` is scored out of 6 determined pairs (v2: 7). `slots_carried`, grouping and the brief are unchanged; v2 readers stay poolable. Every a1 `specific` list is derived from the packet's sources by the rules in `test_score_v2.py`, T14 and T01 included.
+
+**Scoring.** `score_v2.py` applies N1 (strip one pair of `<>` and backticks before comparing names) and C1 (set relation of reader to key: equal / reader-superset / reader-subset / overlap / disjoint, reported beside the existing counts) only when the key names them in `meta.scoring_rules`, as `key_v2_a1.json` does. Scoring against `key_v2.json` is unchanged byte for byte; a test re-renders the filed Claude-v2-A `score.txt` and `score.json` and compares.
+
+**Hand-worked references under a1, before running the scorer.** `specific`: nothing grouped 2 of 6, label-only 2 of 6. `carried`: unchanged, 2 of 7 and 2 of 7. The tests assert these.
+
+**Reporting.** Every v2 reading is scored under both `key_v2.json` and `key_v2_a1.json`, and both are filed. Neither replaces the other; a reading is not re-filed or changed. The Claude-v2-A a1 score is filed beside its v2 score as `score_a1.txt` / `score_a1.json`.
+
+**Direction, stated.** The changes move toward the Claude readings or to n/d. The same rules hold the key against Claude-v2-A at T01, T05, T07, T10 and T12 (twelve names it gave), and R-b was chosen over the alternative that would raise that reading's `specific` pair agreement from 3 of 6 to 4 of 6. That is weak evidence that the rules were not fitted to it: one drafter, one family. A reader from another family is the test.
